            "text": "An execution timeout occurred previously because computing pairwise distances naively over $8000$ high-dimensional vectors incurs massive Python/memory allocation overhead ($>64$ million pairwise distances across dimension $1024$).\n\nWe can formulate the pairwise squared Euclidean distances using matrix multiplication:\n$$\\|x_i - x_j\\|_2^2 = \\|x_i\\|_2^2 + \\|x_j\\|_2^2 - 2 \\langle x_i, x_j \\rangle$$\n\nBy casting to `float64` and leveraging BLAS matrix multiplication (`X @ X.T`), the entire pairwise distance matrix is computed in place with high numerical stability in a few seconds.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n\ndef main():\n    # Load vectors and promote to float64 for numerical precision\n    X = np.load(\"vectors_secondary.npy\").astype(np.float64)\n\n    # Compute squared L2 norms of each row\n    norms = np.sum(X**2, axis=1)\n\n    # Compute Gram matrix using optimized BLAS GEMM: G = X @ X.T\n    G = X @ X.T\n\n    # Compute pairwise squared Euclidean distances in place:\n    # ||x_i - x_j||^2 = ||x_i||^2 + ||x_j||^2 - 2 * <x_i, x_j>\n    G *= -2.0\n    G += norms[:, None]\n    G += norms[None, :]\n\n    # Clamp any slight negative numerical artifacts to 0.0 and take square root\n    np.maximum(G, 0.0, out=G)\n    np.sqrt(G, out=G)\n    np.fill_diagonal(G, 0.0)\n\n    total = float(np.sum(G))\n    print(f\"TOTAL:{total}\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```",
